schema: spec-driven

# radflow-copy: windows
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# radflow quality rules — LOCAL COPY, not a live symlink.
# Canonical source: /d/Dev/radflow/openspec-config.yaml
# This snapshot does NOT auto-update: canonical rule changes will NOT appear
# here until you re-run  /d/Dev/radflow/adopt.sh  inside this repo.
# Reason: Git Bash/MSYS on Windows cannot use symlinks, so adopt.sh copies.
# Repo-specific constraints belong in this repo's own CLAUDE.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

context: |
  Quality rules here are canonical and shared across repos (radflow).
  Binding repo-specific constraints live in this repo's agent memory file
  (CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md, and any docs it points to) and must be restated
  inline in artifacts when relevant — especially in tasks, which must be
  executable without re-deriving them.
  Artifact skeletons live at openspec/templates/ — start every proposal.md,
  design.md, tasks.md, and verification.md from its skeleton instead of
  deriving the structure from these rules.
  These rules are mechanically enforced by ~/dev/radflow/bin/radflow-lint
  (in-session via your harness's hooks, and as a blocking gate on
  `openspec archive`) — artifacts that violate them will be bounced back.
  `openspec archive` additionally requires a verification.md whose Verdict is
  not FAIL — run /opsx-verify before archiving.

# External-review config. KEEP THIS BLOCK FLAT — it is parsed by a single awk
# pass in bin/radflow-lint (parse_review_block, printable via
# `radflow-lint --print-review-tool`): one "key: value" per line, no flow
# mappings ({...}), no anchors, no multi-line scalars. The quorum itself
# (sources, weights, thresholds, arbiter) lives in the peer-review MCP
# server's own peer-review.config.json — radflow only names the tool to call,
# the escalation tier to request per radflow effort tier, and the certainty
# floor below which an achieved verdict warns. tool must be
# [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+ (it is interpolated into grep patterns and messages);
# tierN_escalation keys are positive integers (omit a key = no tier param =
# full escalation). The key selects review depth and cost, not a failover
# barrier: the peer-review server may escalate PAST the requested tier when an
# errored source makes quorum unattainable at that tier (an outage, not a
# disagreement). min_certainty is a 0-1 float. min_caller_agreement is a
# 0-1 float (absent = 0.9): the floor below which an achieved verdict's
# 'Caller Agreement:' value warns — independent peer consensus diverging from
# the drafted design. Defaults when this block is absent: tool
# mcp__peer-review__peer_review, full escalation, min_certainty 0.9,
# min_caller_agreement 0.9.
review:
  tool: mcp__peer-review__peer_review
  tier2_escalation: 1
  tier3_escalation: 2
  min_certainty: 0.9
  min_caller_agreement: 0.9

# Path-risk floors. KEEP THIS BLOCK FLAT — parsed by a single awk pass in
# bin/radflow-lint (parse_risk_block): a block sequence of flat mappings, one
# "key: value" per line, no flow mappings, no multi-line scalars. Each floor
# maps a glob (no spaces) to a minimum tier: a change whose blast radius
# (propose time) or git diff (apply time, --tier-check) touches a matching
# path must declare at least min_tier — declaring below the floor is a
# violation. Globs use shell case(1) matching with '**' folded to '*' ('*'
# crosses '/'). The canonical file ships no floors; per-repo floors belong in
# a detached copy (adopt.sh --detach) or a repo-local $RADFLOW_CONFIG, e.g.:
# risk:
#   floors:
#     - path: src/auth/**
#       min_tier: 2
#     - path: "**/migrations/**"
#       min_tier: 2

rules:
  artifact-parsing:
    - "Artifacts are parsed, not grepped. HTML comment regions (<!-- ... -->) and fenced code blocks are INERT: no check matches a token inside them, so the templates' guidance comments cannot declare anything and an artifact may quote the rules it is written under. Inline code spans (`...`) are inert for markup purposes, so an artifact can write a comment marker or a state word as an example. Carriage returns are stripped before any check runs (a Windows checkout parses identically), and line numbers are preserved (evidence citations and every message carry them)."
    - "An ambiguous parse fails closed, never open: an unterminated HTML comment or code fence is a violation naming the line it opened on — it must never silently make the rest of the file invisible to every check."
    - "A declaration is a FIELD, not a phrase: 'Tier:', 'Type:', 'Mode:', 'External Review:', and the transcribed verdict keys ('Quorum:', 'Certainty:', 'Caller Agreement:', 'Arbiter:', 'Sources:') are recognized only as column-0 'Key: value' lines outside inert regions. Prose that mentions one — a title, a sentence, a rejected alternative — declares nothing."
    - "Field values are matched WHOLE, never by prefix, and a malformed value is a violation rather than a coercion: 'Quorum: achieved' is achieved but 'Quorum: achieved = false (0 of 2 sources)' is a negation, and 'Certainty: 65%' is malformed — not 65, which would silently clear the min_certainty floor. A trailing parenthetical ('achieved (weight 2/2)') is the one tolerated decoration."
    - "The markers 'evidence:', 'correction:', and 'reframed:' are recognized only at the start of a line (after an optional list bullet or indent), on or directly under the task they belong to — not when the token appears mid-sentence in prose or in a task title."
    - "A section runs from its heading to the next heading of the SAME OR SHALLOWER level; a deeper sub-heading does not truncate it (a '## Blast Radius' organized into '### Direct'/'### Indirect' is one section, and its citations are still checked)."
    - "A control is satisfied by recorded WORK, not by the word for it: where a rule requires a hunt, a verdict, or a reframe, the artifact must carry the corresponding section with real body lines — an unfilled '<...>' template token, a placeholder ('TODO'/'TBD'/'n/a'/'none'), or a passing mention of the control's name does not satisfy it."
  proposal:
    - "Open with an effort-tier classification and a one-line justification: Tier 1 (trivial — copy/style/one-file, no behavior fork; may waive external review with a recorded waiver line), Tier 2 (standard behavior change, bounded blast radius), Tier 3 (systemic/architectural/cross-cutting)."
    - "Directly under the tier line, record 'Type: feature' or 'Type: bug' — one word, mechanically checkable. Bug means the change corrects existing behavior; everything else is feature."
    - "A proposal MAY declare the hotfix fast-track lane with a 'Mode: hotfix' line directly under the Type line — one word, mechanically checkable (declared_mode); absent means the normal lane. Mode: hotfix is Tier-2-only: it is a violation on Tier 1 (trivial work uses the review waiver, not a lane) or Tier 3 (systemic work is never fast-tracked past external review), and any Mode value other than 'hotfix' is a violation. A hotfix change whose blast radius (propose time) or diff (--tier-check, apply time) touches a risk: floored path is a violation — risky paths never skip review, however urgent."
    - "Bug-shaped changes require a Root Cause section naming exactly one category — wrong-mental-model | architectural-shortcoming | order-of-operations | missing-edge-case | wrong-abstraction | silent-failure — diagnosed from code actually read (cite files/functions), never from memory of it."
    - "Every change requires a Blast Radius section: a numbered list of every area affected by the same root cause or touched by the change, each entry bearing evidence (file:line or grep hit). An empty blast radius must record the searches that proved it empty (e.g. 'grepped X, Y, Z; no further sites') — never empty by omission. Cited file:line paths are mechanically checked against the repo: a path that never existed is a violation (fabricated or typo'd citation); a stale line number or a since-deleted file warns."
    - "The declared tier is cross-checked mechanically at propose time: the blast radius shape (cited files/dirs) and the root-cause category yield a suggested tier — declaring below it warns; and any cited path matching a risk: floor imposes a minimum tier — declaring below the floor is a violation. At apply time --tier-check re-checks both against the actual git diff."
    - "Creating a change — including one scoped mid-apply for related work discovered while another change is in flight, which gets its own proposal, spec deltas, and tasks once the user approves scoping it — is an openspec/changes/ scaffold, not a git operation: the new change lives on the CURRENT git branch by default. Never create or switch git branches as part of proposing or applying a change; a separate branch happens only when the user explicitly asks for one. (Advisory — judged by /opsx-verify, not radflow-lint.)"
    - "User-stated requirements are prime data: capture every stated design decision, exact label/copy, behavioral spec, and explicit rejection ('do NOT ...') verbatim; if one conflicts with technical reality, document the conflict — never silently drop or reinterpret it. (Advisory — judged by /opsx-verify, not radflow-lint.)"
    - "Consult the repo's openspec/lessons.md — the durable challenging-scenarios log — whenever the blast radius or problem shape overlaps a recorded scenario, and cite the entries consulted; the log exists to accelerate exactly these situations. (Advisory — judged by /opsx-verify, not radflow-lint.)"
  design:
    - "Changes affecting user-facing UI require a UX / Requirements section with verbatim display strings (labels, options, errors, empty states) and an Explicit Rejections list (alternatives considered and why they were rejected). Whether a change is user-facing UI at all is the judgment call. (Advisory — judged by /opsx-verify, not radflow-lint.)"
    - "Explicit Rejections presence is mechanically checked (check_design_sections): a design.md 'UX / Requirements' section with at least one real body line — non-placeholder, not an unfilled '<...>' template token — is the author's own declaration of UI-facing-ness, and once declared, a missing '### Explicit Rejections' subsection inside it, or one with no real body line, is a violation (a control is satisfied by recorded work). An all-placeholder template skeleton does not arm the check, and a design.md with no UX section is not checked."
    - |
      Tier 2 and above follow the External Review protocol, in order (start from openspec/templates/design.md):
      1. Before tasks.md exists, write a <=600-word structured BLIND brief: problem, root cause + category, blast radius, and three questions — how would you approach this? what blast radius would you expect? what risks would you guard? The brief MUST NOT contain the drafted approach — peers answer blind.
      2. Call the config's review tool (the review: block above) once with the brief as the prompt, the drafted file-level approach as callerAnswer (peers never see it — the arbiter rates it against their independent consensus), and tier set to this change's tierN_escalation key (omit tier when the key is unset — full escalation). The tool runs the weighted peer quorum itself and returns a verdict: quorum achieved/not achieved, a 0-1 certaintyScore, a 0-1 callerAgreement, and a per-source roster.
      3. Transcribe the verdict into design.md's 'External Review' section exactly in the template's grammar ('### Peer Review Verdict': Quorum/Certainty/Caller Agreement/Arbiter/Sources lines, with 'Caller Agreement: <0-1|n/a>' — n/a when the tool returned no rating) and record the consensus feedback under '### Review Feedback' verbatim, with a per-point accept/reject rationale. A recorded 'Quorum: not achieved' is an honest record and a HALT, not a pass — transcribe the verdict lines and per-source roster verbatim, incorporate nothing from the review response into the design or the reasoning (no revision, no partial adoption of a responding peer's suggestions), do NOT write tasks.md, and stop and surface the verdict to the user, who alone decides how to proceed (re-run once the outage clears, direct a revision, or explicitly override). A callerAgreement below the config's min_caller_agreement floor means independent peers diverge from the draft — reconcile the divergence in Review Feedback or revise the design. An empty or single-line Review Feedback section earns no pass — record the actual feedback.
      4. Tier 1 instead records 'External Review: Waived — Tier 1 (<one-line rationale>)' in the proposal (a full achieved verdict also passes — a stronger control is never punished). Waiving is Tier-1-only: a 'Waived' line on a Tier 2+ change is a violation, not a substitute for review.
      5. If the peer-review MCP server is genuinely unavailable, record 'External Review: Unavailable — peer-review (mcp__peer-review__peer_review) missing' verbatim (never silently skip); an Unavailable line yields only a degraded (warned, not passing) review — a tracked degradation, not a waiver.
      6. A Tier 2 change on the hotfix fast-track lane (proposal declares 'Mode: hotfix') MAY defer external review: record 'External Review: Deferred — hotfix (<one-line urgency rationale>)' in the proposal (never in tasks.md — an 'External Review: Deferred' field there is a violation; the review debt is tracked by the 'review debt' task instead). On a Tier 2 Mode: hotfix change this satisfies the ordering gate at WARN (a tracked degradation like Unavailable — never a clean pass), and tasks.md MUST carry an unchecked 'review debt' task until the debt is paid by transcribing a full achieved verdict into design.md (which restores a clean pass — a stronger control is never punished). A 'Deferred — hotfix' line on any non-hotfix or non-Tier-2 change is a violation.
    - "Tier 3 dispatches the read-only bomb-squad agent over the blast radius for a latent-defect hunt of the same failure class — in the same message as the peer-review call (parallel tool calls, never serially); record its findings in design.md."
    - "Tier 2 bug fixes whose Root Cause category is wrong-mental-model, architectural-shortcoming, or silent-failure also dispatch bomb-squad, in parallel with the peer-review call — systemic root causes get the latent-defect hunt regardless of declared tier."
    - "A design.md Reframe section with real body lines (unfilled '<...>' template tokens and placeholders are not a recorded reframe) must also carry a 'Lesson: openspec/lessons.md#<slug> — <one-line summary>' marker line — same start-of-line marker grammar as evidence:/correction:/reframed:, recognized ONLY inside the Reframe section and outside inert regions — capturing the challenging scenario durably in the repo's openspec/lessons.md store (created from openspec/templates/lessons.md on first capture; the store's free prose stays outside the document model and is never parsed for trigger tokens). A filled Reframe section with no Lesson: marker warns — a signal, not a verdict, the same posture as the two-corrections warn. An empty or placeholder payload ('TODO'/'TBD'/'n/a'/a template '<...>' token), or a payload that does not cite openspec/lessons.md#<slug>, is a violation — a capture satisfied by the token alone is no capture. On a live change tree (the change dir at <repo>/openspec/changes/, same gating and RADFLOW_EVIDENCE_CHECK=0 kill-switch as evidence paths) a cited slug with no matching '## <slug>' heading in openspec/lessons.md is a violation (fabricated or typo'd capture)."
  tasks:
    - "Do not generate tasks.md until design.md's External Review section records an achieved peer-review verdict (or the proposal records a Tier 1 waiver, or a Tier 2 Mode: hotfix change records the 'External Review: Deferred — hotfix (<rationale>)' line and tasks.md carries an unchecked 'review debt' task)."
    - "Task wording is mechanically checked: the phrases 'basic fix' and 'initial support' (case-insensitive) on a task's own lines — the checkbox line or its continuation lines, outside inert regions and outside evidence:/correction:/reframed: marker payloads — draw a warning naming the line and phrase (check_tasks). A warn, never a violation: phrase-matching natural language is a heuristic — a signal, not a verdict. Phrases outside any task block are prose and stay silent."
    - "Each task must be self-contained — implementable from tasks.md alone: restate binding constraints from the repo's CLAUDE.md inline, reproduce UX strings verbatim, and state measurable done-when criteria. (Advisory — judged by /opsx-verify, not radflow-lint.)"
    - "Checkboxes are the contract: every task carries explicit verification steps and a box is ticked only after its verification actually ran. Tasks touching user-facing UI require a browser-verification step driven via the playwright-cli skill against a live dev server — typecheck/curl is not a substitute. (The browser-verification clause is advisory — judged by /opsx-verify, not radflow-lint.)"
    - |
      A box may only be ticked with an evidence line appended on or directly under the task:
      1. 'evidence: <file:line>' for code changes, or the exact test/command run and its result for verification steps.
      2. A tick without evidence is invalid; placeholder evidence ('evidence: TODO', 'TBD', 'n/a', or an empty payload) is a tick without evidence — a violation.
      3. Cited file:line paths are mechanically checked against the repo: a path that never existed is a violation (fabricated or typo'd citation); a stale line number or a since-deleted file warns.
      4. Fabricated evidence (a file:line that does not contain the claimed change) is an automatic verification FAIL regardless of tier.
    - "Task terminal states are DONE, FAIL, BLOCKED, or OBSOLETE only, and a state is a POSITION, not a word: it is recognized as a bare uppercase token on the task's own checkbox line, a dash- or parenthesis-introduced capitalized marker on that line ('— Deferred to next sprint', '(Deferred)'), or a 'status:'/'state:' value under it. 'Deferred' is not a state — deferred work becomes an unchecked follow-up task or a follow-up change — but the word in ordinary prose (a task description, an evidence:/correction:/reframed: payload, a comment, a code fence) is legal: the rule bans the state, not the vocabulary. An 'External Review: Deferred' field in tasks.md remains a violation (the hotfix state line belongs in proposal.md)."
    - "During apply, a user course-correction is recorded as a 'correction: <one-line summary of what the user corrected>' line on or directly under the affected task — the same placement grammar as evidence: lines. An empty or placeholder payload ('TODO', 'TBD', 'n/a', or a template '<...>' token) is a violation, consistent with placeholder evidence."
    - "When the user corrects the same shortcoming a second time, stop implementing and run the reframe protocol (/opsx-reframe) before ticking further boxes. Judging whether two corrections address the same shortcoming — including across different tasks — is a judgment call. (Advisory — judged by /opsx-verify, not radflow-lint.) The mechanical proxy: a non-terminal task (not OBSOLETE, not FAIL) carrying two or more correction: lines and no reframed: line warns (a signal, not a verdict), naming /opsx-reframe."
    - "A reframe concludes in exactly one of two recorded ways per affected task: the superseded task is marked OBSOLETE and replaced by reframed tasks, or the surviving task gains a 'reframed: <design.md Reframe pointer — one-line summary>' line with rewritten done-when criteria. Either clears the corrections warn; an empty or placeholder reframed: payload is a violation. design.md records the reframe in a '## Reframe' section: the wrong assumption, the corrected model, and the approach delta."
    - "Atypical reasoning effort is not mechanically observable (the linter has no clocks); its observable proxies are the artifact grammar. Capture a lesson entry in openspec/lessons.md even without a reframe when observable grind occurs — a task reaching FAIL or BLOCKED, a single correction that lands deep, or repeated failed verification attempts. (Advisory — judged by /opsx-verify, not radflow-lint.)"
